## Final Judgement
The fact-check reveals that Professor Paul Lehner's statement was taken out of context—he described the idea as a speculative last-resort scenario, not an endorsement. Crucially, the scientific consensus, as expressed in a *Lancet* letter signed by 80 scientists and by the World Health Organization, explicitly rejects the notion that deliberately exposing people to COVID-19 to achieve herd immunity is effective or ethical, calling it a "dangerous fallacy" and "unethical." There is no peer-reviewed evidence supporting the claim; instead, evidence shows that children face documented risks of severe illness, hospitalization, and multisystem inflammatory syndrome. Historical "pox parties" for chickenpox are not medically acceptable and carry significant risks. Therefore, the claim that deliberately infecting children at "pox parties" could be a good way to create herd immunity is clearly contradicted by authoritative sources.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim misrepresents Professor Paul Lehner's statement, which was a speculative remark about a hypothetical last-resort scenario, not an endorsement ([Daily Mail](https://www.dailymail.co.uk/news/article-8868269/Controversial-pox-parties-used-build-immunity-Covid-19-scientist-says.html)). The scientific consensus, including a *Lancet* letter signed by 80 scientists and the World Health Organization, explicitly rejects deliberate infection for herd immunity as a "dangerous fallacy" and "unethical" ([NBC News](https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/covid-19-herd-immunity-backed-white-house-dangerous-fallacy-scientists-n1243415), [The Guardian](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/oct/12/who-chief-says-herd-immunity-approach-to-pandemic-unethical)). Children face documented risks of severe illness, hospitalization, and multisystem inflammatory syndrome from COVID-19 ([WHO](https://www.who.int/news-room/commentaries/detail/multisystem-inflammatory-syndrome-in-children-and-adolescents-with-covid-19)), and historical "pox parties" for chickenpox carry significant risks and are not medically acceptable ([Immunization Info](https://immunizationinfo.com/what-is-a-chickenpox-party/)).